#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Tell the operator what to DO about the wrap desk, hourly, until it is done.

This is not a health check. It is a task list that nags. Every wrap has manual
steps spread over hours -- wait for confirmations, then send wPCN -- and a step
forgotten halfway is how a desk ends up with a customer paid nothing.

There is no manual buy-back step any more. Until 2026-09-08 each wrap ended
"sell the PCN, buy wPCN back from the pool", read from the keeper's KEEPER_BUY
flag (keeper_buys(), now removed). Since 2026-09-25 the keeper holds the pool
to the PCN INDEX in both directions by itself, every minute, within its daily
budget, so this script only reports how far the pool is from the index -- the
peg's temperature, never an instruction to trade by hand.

WHAT "DONE" MEANS HERE.

Nothing is marked complete by a human saying so. Each step is derived from what
is observable on a chain:

    PCN arrived at the reserve        -> explorer.pc.am / the node
    it has 100 confirmations          -> same
    wPCN was sent to the requester    -> the BSC receipt for the send
    wPCN was bought back              -> the pool's wPCN reserve fell

This paragraph used to end "and that is why there is no button", on the grounds
that a button lets an operator mark a step done that did not happen. The premise
was right and the conclusion did not follow: what makes a claim safe is that it
is CHECKED, not that it is typed on a server. So --released now reads the BSC
receipt for the hash it is given and refuses every hash that does not show the
right amount of wPCN reaching the right address. That is a stronger guarantee
than the SSH-only version ever had -- it accepted any string, including none.

An unreadable answer is still UNKNOWN, and unknown still never becomes done: if
the BSC RPC cannot be reached, --released refuses and says so rather than taking
the operator's word for it.

That cuts both ways, deliberately. If a read FAILS, the task stays open and gets
nagged again. Better to be told twice about something finished than to have a
half-finished wrap go quiet.

Alerts go to the PRIVATE ops channel via pcoin-notify. Never the public one.
"""
